    {
      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this fragment carefully.\n\nThis is an OIML Certificate:\n- Number: R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01 revision 13\n- Project number: 3939609\n- Page total: 13\n- Member State: The Netherlands\n- Scheme: A\n- Issuing authority: NMi Certin B.V., OIML Issuing Authority NL1\n- Person responsible: M.Ph.D. Schmidt\n- Date issued: 5 June 2025\n- Address: Thijsseweg 11, 2629 JA Delft, The Netherlands\n- Phone: +31 88 636 2332\n- Email: certin@nmi.nl\n- Website: www.nmi.nl\n- oiml_issuer_id: NL1\n\nApplicant and Manufacturer (combined):\n- ABB Limited, Oldends Lane, GL10 3TA Stonehouse, United Kingdom\n\nCertified type:\n- Category: Water meter (electromagnetic water meter)\n- Type: AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4*\n- X: 1 (standard), 3 (advanced), 5 (premium / mobile comms MC)\n- Y: 1, 2, 8, or 9 (integral/remote version, optionally sensor only)\n- Z: R (reduced bore), V (virtual full bore), F (full bore)\n\nRecommendation:\n- R 49-1 (2013) — Water meters intended for the metering of cold potable water and hot water\n- id canonical: R49, edition: 2013? Wait — \"R 49-1 (2013)\" — R49-1 is part 1 of R49. Edition 2013. Accuracy classes: 1 and 2 → accuracy_classes: [1, 2]? Hmm, for water meters, accuracy class 1 and 2. Yes.\n\nActually, the schema says recommendation.id canonical e.g. \"R60\" not \"OIML R 60\". For R 49-1, I'd use \"R49-1\" as id, edition 2013.\n\nTest reports:\n- NMi-2254799-01 dated 14 August 2020, 235 pages\n- NMi-2254799-03 dated 30 September 2020, 44 pages\n- NMi-2254799-04 dated 24 December 2020, 180 pages\n- NMi-2564429-01 dated 2 August 2021, 26 pages\n- NMi-2496280-01 dated 26 August 2021, 29 pages\n- NMi-2496280-02 dated 23 November 2021, 25 pages\n- NMi-2563125-01 dated 22 September 2023, 94 pages\n\nRole: these are the \"associated reports\" — likely type evaluation reports. The certificate says \"The conformity was established by the results of tests and examinations provided in the associated reports\". Also \"Documentation folder no. T11851-9\" — that's a documentation_file component... Actually test_reports[] role: documentation_file for T11851-9? The schema lists test_reports with role: type_evaluation_report | test_report | documentation_file | evaluation_report. The Documentation folder no. T11851-9 could be recorded as a test_report with role \"documentation_file\". Hmm, it says \"The construction of the measuring instrument is recorded in the Documentation folder no. T11851-9.\" I'll include it as role documentation_file.\n\nThe reports - role type_evaluation_report seems appropriate (they are the OIML Type Evaluation Report(s) mentioned). The first one NMi-2254799-01 probably is the Type Evaluation Report. The others are likely additional test reports. Since we can't be sure, I'll mark the first as type_evaluation_report and others as test_report. Actually the cert says \"the sample(s) identified in the OIML Type Evaluation Report(s)\" — plural. Hard to determine. I'll mark all as type_evaluation_report? Hmm. Safer: the first one (which \"includes 235 pages\") is the TER; the later-dated ones are test reports/additions. Let me mark NMi-2254799-01 as type_evaluation_report and the rest as test_report. Actually NMi-2254799-03 and -04 share the same prefix — they might be parts of the evaluation. I'll use type_evaluation_report for the 2254799 series and test_report for the others. That's a reasonable interpretation.\n\nCharacteristics tables:\n\nTable 1: General characteristics for AquaMaster4 FEW41Y.Z and FEW43Y.Z\n- Measuring principle: Electromagnetic\n- Accuracy class: 1 and 2\n- Environmental class: O (installed outdoors)\n- Electromagnetic environment: E2\n- Mechanical class: M1\n- Temperature range ambient: -25 °C / +55 °C\n- Water temperature class: T50 (+0.1 °C / +50 °C)\n- Maximum admissible pressure (MAP): 1.6 MPa (16 bar)\n- Orientation: All positions\n- Flow profile sensitivity class: U0 and D0, except full bore DN250+: U3 and D0\n- Reverse flow: sensor intended to measure reverse flow\n- Pressure loss class: varies by bore and DN\n- Power supply: AC mains 95–240 VAC @ 50/60 Hz; renewable DC 6–32 V; replaceable battery 2x 3.6 V (2.9–3.6 V)\n- Software identification: table 3\n- Checking facilities: Type P (permanent)\n\nTable 2: same for FEW45Y.Z, differences:\n- Electromagnetic environment: E1 for transmitter with external antenna cable / E2 without\n- Power supply: AC mains 95–240 VAC @ 50/60 Hz incl 4x 3.6 V batteries; renewable 6–32 V incl 4x 3.6 V batteries; replaceable battery 6x 3.6 V (2.9–3.6 V)\n\nModel family: The cert covers variants FEW41Y.Z, FEW43Y.Z, FEW45Y.Z, FET4* (sensor). Models vary by bore (reduced/virtual full/full) and DN size. The fragment only has Tables 1 and 2 (general characteristics for transmitter types). Tables 5–12 (with DN-specific data) are on later pages not in this fragment.\n\nHow should I structure this? The fragment includes:\n- Certificate metadata\n- Issuing authority\n- Applicant/manufacturer\n- Certified type\n- Recommendation\n- Test reports\n- Tables 1 and 2 characteristics\n- Reference to tables 3–12 and documentation folder\n\nModel family: The type designations mention families: FEW4XY.Z (flow transmitter variants) and FET4* (flow sensor?). Actually FET4 is the flow sensor (electromagnetic flowtube). X∈{1,3,5}, Y∈{1,2,8,9}, Z∈{R,V,F}.\n\nI could define models based on transmitter types: FEW41Y.Z, FEW43Y.Z, FEW45Y.Z. Since Tables 1 & 2 differ per transmitter type (FEW41/FEW43 same; FEW45 differs), I could do model_level entries.\n\nBut the instructions say not to overcomplicate. Let me think about what's most useful and faithful.\n\nApproach:\n- certificate-level characteristics.type_level: attributes common to the type (from tables 1 & 2, which are mostly identical): measuring principle, accuracy class, environmental class, mechanical class, temperature range, water temperature class, MAP, orientation, flow profile sensitivity class, reverse flow, checking facilities.\n- Differences between FEW41/43 and FEW45: electromagnetic environment, power supply (battery count). These could be model_level with models \"FEW41Y.Z\", \"FEW43Y.Z\", \"FEW45Y.Z\".\n\nmodel_family:\n- family_name: \"AquaMaster4\"\n- models: The type is parameterized: X ∈ {1,3,5} and Z ∈ {R,V,F}, Y ∈ {1,2,8,9}. That's a lot of combos. Maybe model entries per transmitter type: FEW41Y.Z, FEW43Y.Z, FEW45Y.Z, and FET4* sensor. Hmm.\n\nActually the models listed in Tables 1-2 grouping: \"FEW41Y.Z and FEW43Y.Z\" (Table 1, Table 3) and \"FEW45Y.Z\" (Table 2, Table 4). So the model axis at this level is the transmitter type. I'll create models:\n- \"FEW41Y.Z\" (label: standard, X=1)\n- \"FEW43Y.Z\" (label: advanced, X=3)\n- \"FEW45Y.Z\" (label: premium / mobile comms MC, X=5)\n- \"FET4*\" flow sensor variants — hmm, FET4 is mentioned in type designation: \"Type: AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4*\". FET4 likely the flow sensor (tube). The attributes table refers to \"flow sensor and flow transmitter\".\n\nMaybe components: \n- flow transmitter: FEW4XY.Z\n- flow sensor: FET4*\n\nActually AquaMaster4 is a system consisting of flow sensor (FET4) + transmitter (FEW4). The cert says \"for both flow sensor and flow transmitter\". I'll add components:\n- {role: \"flow_transmitter\", type_designations: [\"FEW41Y.Z\",\"FEW43Y.Z\",\"FEW45Y.Z\"], characteristics...}\n- {role: \"flow_sensor\", type_designations: [\"FET4\"], ...}\n\nHmm, but be careful about fabricating. The cert identifies the type as \"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4*\". It's reasonable to list them as the type designations. Components: the schema says include only if cert names sub-assemblies. The tables do name \"flow sensor\" and \"flow transmitter\". I think it's justified.\n\nActually, maybe safer to keep components minimal or omit. The type designations FEW4XY.Z and FET4* are in certified_type. The tables reference \"flow sensor and flow transmitter\" generically. I could include components with role \"flow_transmitter\" (FEW4XY.Z variants) and \"flow_sensor\" (FET4). I'll include it — it's grounded in the text.\n\nCharacteristics — how to encode power supply:\n\"AC mains: 95–240 VAC @ 50/60 Hz\" →\n- power_supply_voltage_ac: {min:95, max:240} V\n- power_supply_frequency: {min:50, max:60} Hz\n- power_supply_type: \"AC mains\" — for table 2 it's \"AC mains ... including 4x 3.6 V replaceable batteries\"\n- battery: \"2x 3,6 V (operating range 2,9–3,6 V)\" → replaceable_battery_count: 2, battery_voltage: 3.6 V, battery_operating_range: {min:2.9, max:3.6} V\n\nFor table 2: battery count 6.\n\n\"Renewable energy power (DC powered): 6–32 V\" → power_supply_voltage_dc: {min:6, max:32} V.\n\nElectromagnetic environment differs: FEW41/43: E2; FEW45: E1 with external antenna cable, E2 without. So model_level entry:\n- attribute: electromagnetic_environment, values: [{model: FEW41Y.Z, value: \"E2\"}, {model: FEW43Y.Z, value: \"E2\"}, {model: FEW45Y.Z, value: \"E1 (with external antenna cable) / E2 (without)\"}]. Hmm — value should be a string here since it's a class, that's fine.\n\nActually for FEW45, maybe config_level: axis \"external antenna cable\". Could do: values [{condition: \"with external antenna cable\", value: \"E1\"}, {condition: \"without external antenna cable\", value: \"E2\"}]. That's nice. But mixing model and config... The schema allows model_level values with footnote markers but condition is config_level. I'll keep it simple: model_level for FEW45 with value string \"E1 (with external antenna cable), E2 (without external antenna cable)\". Or two entries. Let me do model_level with a string value.\n\nAlternatively, put electromagnetic environment in type_level as \"E2 (E1 for FEW45 transmitter with external antenna cable)\" — no, that's messy.\n\nSince Tables 1 and 2 differ only in electromagnetic environment and power supply battery details, I'll:\n- type_level: all common attributes (measuring_principle, accuracy_class, environmental_class, mechanical_class, ambient_temperature_range, water_temperature_class, maximum_admissible_pressure, orientation, flow_profile_sensitivity_class, reverse_flow, checking_facilities, pressure_loss_class).\n- model_level: electromagnetic_environment, replaceable_battery_count (2 vs 6).\n\nWait, power supply for FEW45 also says AC mains and DC include 4x3.6 V replaceable batteries — a note. Battery count for pure battery operation: 2x (Table 1) vs 6x (Table 2). So replaceable_battery_count model_level: FEW41Y.Z: 2, FEW43Y.Z: 2, FEW45Y.Z: 6.\n\nPressure loss class: varies by bore type and DN size. This is a config-level thing:\n- attribute: pressure_loss_class, axis: bore_type & size.\nCould represent as config_level entries:\n- {attribute: \"pressure_loss_class\", axis: \"bore_type/size\", values: [{condition: \"reduced_bore DN40–DN50\", value: \"Δp 40 (0.40 bar)\"}, {condition: \"reduced_bore DN>50\", value: \"Δp 63 (0.63 bar)\"}, {condition: \"virtual/full bore DN40–DN50\", value: \"Δp 10 (0.10 bar)\"}, {condition: \"virtual/full bore DN65–DN200\", value: \"Δp 16 (0.16 bar)\"}, {condition: \"virtual/full bore DN250+\", value: \"Δp 10 (0.10 bar)\"}]}\n\nBut should I include pressure_loss at type level? The rules say no LaTeX etc. Δp is Unicode delta — allowed? The rules say never include LaTeX ($...$, \\mathrm etc.). Unicode Δp is fine I think, but to be safe I can write \"pressure_loss_class\" values as strings like \"Δp 40 (0.40 bar)\". Hmm, or value: 0.40, unit \"bar\" with condition carrying the class. Let me do value: 0.4, unit_symbol: \"bar\", with condition text including \"Δp 40\". Actually the condition should describe the config axis: e.g. condition \"reduced_bore, DN40–DN50\". The Δp class label can be in the value string or a separate field. Values follow the same structured form {condition, value, unit_symbol}. So:\n\nconfig_level entries:\n- attribute: \"pressure_loss\", axis: \"bore_type_and_nominal_size\", values: [\n  {condition: \"Reduced bore DN40–DN50 (Δp 40)\", value: 0.40, unit_symbol: \"bar\"},\n  {condition: \"Reduced bore > DN50 (Δp 63)\", value: 0.63, unit_symbol: \"bar\"},\n  {condition: \"Virtual or full bore DN40–DN50 (Δp 10)\", value: 0.10, unit_symbol: \"bar\"},\n  {condition: \"Virtual or full bore DN65–DN200 (Δp 16)\", value: 0.16, unit_symbol: \"bar\"},\n  {condition: \"Virtual or full bore DN250 and larger (Δp 10)\", value: 0.10, unit_symbol: \"bar\"}\n]\n\nNote Table 1 is for FEW41/43 and Table 2 for FEW45; both have same pressure loss. Fine at type level.\n\nFlow profile sensitivity: \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream/downstream), except full bore sizes DN250 and larger: U3 and D0\". Config-level:\n- attribute: \"flow_profile_sensitivity_class\", axis: \"bore_type_and_nominal_size\", values: [{condition: \"default (full bore below DN250 and reduced bore)\", value: \"U0 / D0\"}, {condition: \"full bore DN250 and larger\", value: \"U3 / D0\"}]. Hmm, \"0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream\". I'll include.\n\nActually to keep it manageable, maybe keep flow_profile_sensitivity_class at type_level as string \"U0 and D0 (0×DN upstream, 0×DN downstream); full bore DN250 and larger: U3 and D0\". Values must be structured objects... type_level format: `<label>: {value, unit_symbol, footnote_markers[]}` — value can be a string. So a descriptive string is OK.\n\nSimilarly pressure loss could be a string at type level, but the config_level representation is richer. I'll use config_level for pressure loss.\n\nHmm, but wait — actually I should be careful: rules say \"Lists of values ... is a MODEL MATRIX\". Not applicable here.\n\nWater temperature class: \"T50 (+0,1 °C / +50 °C)\" → water_temperature_class: \"T50\", with range {min: 0.1, max: 50} °C. I'll do:\n- water_temperature_class: {value: \"T50\"}\n- water_temperature_range: {value: {min: 0.1, max: 50}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}\n\nMAP: 1.6 MPa (16 bar) → maximum_admissible_pressure: {value: 1.6, unit_symbol: \"MPa\"}. Could add secondary \"maximum_admissible_pressure_bar: 16 bar\"? That's redundant; maybe include as one value with unit MPa. I'll include just MPa. Or both? I'll do MPa only.\n\nAmbient temperature: {min: -25, max: 55} °C.\n\nPower supply — model differences. Let me structure:\n\ntype_level:\n- measuring_principle: {value: \"Electromagnetic\"}\n- accuracy_class: {value: \"1 and 2\"} — hmm, values \"1\", \"2\". Could be value: [1,2]? Schema says value: number | {min,max} | string. A list isn't allowed. So string \"1 and 2\" or record as recommendation.accuracy_classes [1,2] too. I'll use string \"1 and 2\" — hmm, maybe \"1, 2\"? The certificate header says \"Accuracy class — 1 / 2\". I'll use value: \"1 and 2\" for table-derived and recommendation accuracy_classes: [1, 2].\n\n- environmental_class: {value: \"O (installed outdoors)\"} → value \"O\", maybe include note. I'll do value: \"O\", and add environment_installation: \"installed outdoors\"? Simpler: value: \"O (installed outdoors)\". Strings allowed. Fine.\n\n- mechanical_class: {value: \"M1\"}\n- electromagnetic_environment: model_level (differences).\n- ambient_temperature_range: {min:-25, max:55} °C\n- water_temperature_class: \"T50\"; water_temperature_range: {0.1, 50} °C\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: 1.6 MPa\n- orientation: \"All positions (horizontal, vertical, or diagonal)\"\n- reverse_flow: \"The sensor is intended to measure reverse flow\" → maybe boolean-ish string \"intended to measure reverse flow\"? I'll keep string \"measured (sensor intended to measure reverse flow)\"? Simpler: reverse_flow: {value: \"measured\"} — hmm, don't fabricate. Keep the string as-is minus punctuation: \"The sensor is intended to measure reverse flow\". Fine.\n\n- checking_facilities: {value: \"Type P (permanent) automatic checking facility\"}\n- software_identification: \"See details in table 3 / table 4\" — omit or mention? It's a cross-reference; omit from characteristics.\n\nmodel_level:\n- electromagnetic_environment: [{model FEW41Y.Z, value \"E2\"}, {FEW43Y.Z, \"E2\"}, {FEW45Y.Z, value: \"E1 / E2 depending on antenna cable\"}]. Hmm. Let me think again — better: FEW45Y.Z value: \"E1 (with external antenna cable) or E2 (without external antenna cable)\". Strings allowed.\n\nActually, cleaner: two model_level entries? No — one entry per attribute with values array. OK.\n\n- replaceable_battery_count: [{FEW41Y.Z, 2}, {FEW43Y.Z, 2}, {FEW45Y.Z, 6}] — unit null. And battery_voltage 3.6 V (both tables, count-specific \"2x 3,6 V\" / \"6x 3,6 V\"). battery_operating_range {2.9–3.6} V.\n\nPower supply voltage/frequency are same across models (95–240 VAC, 50/60 Hz; DC 6–32 V) → type_level:\n- ac_power_supply_voltage: {min:95, max:240} V\n- ac_power_supply_frequency: {min:50, max:60} Hz\n- dc_power_supply_voltage: {min:6, max:32} V (renewable energy / DC powered)\n- battery_voltage: 3.6 V\n- battery_operating_voltage_range: {min:2.9, max:3.6} V\n\nNote Table 2 note: AC/DC includes 4x 3.6 V replaceable batteries — this is a model-specific note; I could capture in model attributes as auxiliary_battery_count... That may be overkill. I'll skip or include as string in the FEW45 model attributes? The schema's models[] have attributes{} free-form. I could put attributes on models: {\"electromagnetic_environment\": \"E1/E2\", ...}. But rules put varying attributes in model_level. I'll use model_level.\n\nComponents: Should I add? Let me consider: the certified type = \"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4*\". The tables mention \"flow sensor and flow transmitter\". Components:\n- {role: \"flow transmitter\", type_designations: [\"FEW41Y.Z\", \"FEW43Y.Z\", \"FEW45Y.Z\"]}\n- {role: \"flow sensor\", type_designations: [\"FET4\"]}\n\nHmm — actually FET4XY.Z? The type says \"FET4 *\" with X/Y/Z meaning same wildcard? The text: \"Type: AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4 \\*\". So FET4* with the same X/Y/Z meanings. So the sensor type designations: FET41Y.Z, FET43Y.Z, FET45Y.Z? The wildcard expansion presumably applies. I'll list \"FET4*\" as designation and note the wildcard meaning in the type description.\n\ncertified_type:\n- category: \"Water meter\"\n- type_designations: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"FET4*\", \"FEW41Y.Z\", \"FEW43Y.Z\", \"FEW45Y.Z\"]? The rules say \"all type/model identifiers mentioned\". The explicit ones: AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z, FET4*. Tables mention FEW41Y.Z, FEW43Y.Z, FEW45Y.Z. I'll include all: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"AquaMaster4 FET4*\", \"FEW41Y.Z\", \"FEW43Y.Z\", \"FEW45Y.Z\"]. Hmm, maybe simpler: [\"FEW4XY.Z\", \"FET4*\", \"FEW41Y.Z\", \"FEW43Y.Z\", \"FEW45Y.Z\"]. I'll include \"AquaMaster4\" family name in model_family.\n\n- module_designation: null (not mentioned)\n- description: electromagnetic water meter; wildcard meanings. Include description: \"Electromagnetic water meter. X = 1 (standard), 3 (advanced), 5 (premium, mobile comms MC); Y = 1, 2, 8 or 9 (integral/remote version, optionally sensor only); Z = R (reduced bore), V (virtual full bore) or F (full bore).\"\n\nmodel_family:\n- family_name: \"AquaMaster4\"\n- models: Which variants? Based on tables: transmitter types FEW41Y.Z, FEW43Y.Z (Table 1) and FEW45Y.Z (Table 2). But Y and Z are wildcards, so full enumeration would be huge (3 X values × 4 Y × 3 Z = 36 transmitter variants + sensors). The tables group by X. I think models should be at the level the certificate distinguishes: FEW41Y.Z, FEW43Y.Z, FEW45Y.Z. Attributes: {\"x_meaning\": \"standard\"|\"advanced\"|\"premium (mobile comms MC)\"}.\n\nAlternatively define axes: x_variant, y_variant, z_variant. But rules prefer models[] entries. I'll do 3 models with labels.\n\nHmm — but the model_level characteristics reference model ids \"FEW41Y.Z\" etc. Consistent.\n\nWait — should Y variants also be models? \"Y being 1, 2, 8 or 9 (integral/remote version, optionally sensor only)\". The certificate doesn't tabulate differences per Y in this fragment. I'll note in model attributes/description but not enumerate. Actually, maybe I could add models for Y? That would explode. Keep the 3 transmitter-type models and mention wildcard axes in attributes.\n\nAlternatively, models[]:\n- {model_id: \"FEW41Y.Z\", label: \"AquaMaster4 standard transmitter\", attributes: {x: 1, x_meaning: \"standard\"}}\n- {model_id: \"FEW43Y.Z\", label: \"AquaMaster4 advanced transmitter\", attributes: {x: 3, x_meaning: \"advanced\"}}\n- {model_id: \"FEW45Y.Z\", label: \"AquaMaster4 premium transmitter (mobile comms MC)\", attributes: {x: 5, x_meaning: \"premium (mobile comms MC)\"}}\n\nAnd maybe attribute wildcard_axes: {Y: \"1, 2, 8 or 9 (integral/remote, optionally sensor only)\", Z: \"R (reduced bore), V (virtual full bore), F (full bore)\"} at family level? model_family schema has family_name and models[] only... \"attributes{}\" is per model. I could add the wildcard info to each model's attributes or to the type description. The description already covers it. Fine.\n\nmatrix_tables: Tables 5–12 (with DN sizes, Q3 etc.) are on pages 5+ — not in this fragment. So no matrix_tables here. Wait, but the fragment includes pages 1–4. Tables 3+ content begins at end (\"Table 3 Software identification ...\" header, but no table content). So no matrix tables with data.\n\nShould I create matrix_tables from Table 1/2? They're 2-column key-value tables, not multi-dim. The characteristics section covers them. No matrix_tables needed.\n\nrevision_history: \"revision 13\" — only current revision, no history. The schema revision_history wants changes; we only know revision 13, date 5 June 2025? The date_issued is 5 June 2025, and number \"revision 13\". I could add revision_history: [{revision: \"13\", date: \"2025-06-05\", changes: null}]. Hmm, revision as string \"13\". The rule example: revision (string, e.g. \"0\" not 0). I'll include with date 2025-06-05 and changes omitted (don't fabricate). Actually is it safe to link the date? The date appears near \"Issuing Authority — NMi Certin B.V., OIML Issuing Authority NL1 / 5 June 2025\". It's the certificate issue date. Reasonable. But changes field — omit. I might just include revision_history: [{\"revision\": \"13\", \"date\": \"2025-06-05\"}]. Or omit entirely to avoid noise? I think including revision in certificate: number already includes \"revision 13\"? The number field: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" and revision 13 separate. I'll put certificate.number = \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" and add revision_history entry. Fine.\n\ncertificate:\n- number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\"\n- scheme: \"A\"\n- project_number: \"3939609\"\n- page_total: 13\n- member_state: \"The Netherlands\"\n- date_issued: \"2025-06-05\"\n- oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\"\n\nissuing_authority:\n- name: \"NMi Certin B.V.\"\n- address_lines: [\"Thijsseweg 11\", \"2629 JA Delft\", \"The Netherlands\"]\n- person_responsible: \"M.Ph.D. Schmidt\"\n- person_title: \"Certification Board\" — hmm, \"Certification Board\" appears above NMi Certin B.V. signature block. Person title? M.Ph.D. Schmidt is person responsible; the signature block says \"Certification Board\" then NMi Certin B.V. Possibly Schmidt signs for the Certification Board. person_title: \"Certification Board\"? It's plausible. I'll include person_title: \"Certification Board\". Hmm, risk of misattribution. The text:\n\n\"Issuing Authority — NMi Certin B.V., OIML Issuing Authority NL1\n5 June 2025\nCertification Board\nNMi Certin B.V.\nThijsseweg 11...\"\n\nLooks like the signature block: date, \"Certification Board\" (title), organization. I'll include it.\n\n- phone: \"+31 88 636 2332\"\n- email: \"certin@nmi.nl\"\n- website: \"www.nmi.nl\"\n- oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\"\n\napplicants: [{name: \"ABB Limited\", address_lines: [\"Oldends Lane\", \"GL10 3TA Stonehouse\", \"United Kingdom\"]}]\n\nmanufacturers: same — \"Applicant and Manufacturer — ABB Limited ...\". So manufacturers: same entry.\n\ncertified_type:\n- category: \"Water meter\" (the doc says \"water meter\"; recommendation R49 water meters). \n- type_designations: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"AquaMaster4 FET4*\"] plus explicit variants FEW41Y.Z, FEW43Y.Z, FEW45Y.Z. I'll include: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"AquaMaster4 FET4*\", \"FEW41Y.Z\", \"FEW43Y.Z\", \"FEW45Y.Z\"]. Hmm \"FET4 \\*\" → \"FET4*\". \n- module_designation: null\n- description: \"Electromagnetic water meter (AquaMaster4) comprising flow transmitter FEW4XY.Z and flow sensor FET4*. X: 1 = standard, 3 = advanced, 5 = premium (mobile comms MC). Y: 1, 2, 8 or 9 (integral/remote version, optionally sensor only). Z: R = reduced bore, V = virtual full bore, F = full bore.\"\n\ncomponents: \n- {role: \"flow transmitter\", type_designations: [\"FEW4XY.Z (FEW41Y.Z, FEW43Y.Z, FEW45Y.Z)\"]} — hmm, keep type_designations as list: [\"FEW41Y.Z\", \"FEW43Y.Z\", \"FEW45Y.Z\"]. alternatives: null.\n- {role: \"flow sensor\", type_designations: [\"FET4*\"]}\n\nIs this fabrication? The type identification says \"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4*\" — two sub-types. Tables 1/2 explicitly reference \"flow sensor and flow transmitter\". I think it's grounded enough. I'll include.\n\nHmm, wait — is FEW the transmitter and FET the sensor? FEW = Flow... transmitter? Actually in ABB AquaMaster: \"AquaMaster3 FEW...\" hmm. FET = Flow ElectroMagnetic Tube? The tables say \"with transmitter types FEW41Y.Z and FEW43Y.Z\" — Table 1 title: \"General characteristics for AquaMaster4 FEW41Y.Z and AquaMaster4 FEW43Y.Z\"; page 2: \"General characteristics of AquaMaster4 with transmitter types FEW41Y.Z and FEW43Y.Z\". So FEW* are transmitters. Then FET4* is the flow sensor (tube). Yes: FET = Flow tube. And \"for both flow sensor and flow transmitter\" temperature. Also Table 9-12 \"indicating device\". So components: flow sensor FET4*, flow transmitter FEW4XY.Z, indicating device (built into transmitter?). The indicating device tables exist but that's later pages. I'll include indicating device? Not named with type designation in this fragment; the tables 9-12 are referenced by name. I'll skip indicating device component or include with no designation? Better skip — don't fabricate.\n\nrecommendation:\n- id: \"R49-1\"\n- edition: 2013\n- amendment: null\n- scheme: \"A\"\n- accuracy_classes: [1, 2]? Hmm — accuracy class for water meters R49: class 1 and 2. In JSON: [1, 2]. Hmm, but accuracy classes as numbers is fine.\n\nActually wait — is \"R 49-1 (2013)\" edition 2013? Yes. id canonical: \"R49-1\"? The example canonicalizes \"OIML R 60\" → \"R60\". R 49-1 is a part. \"R49-1\" seems right.\n\ntest_reports:\n- {id: \"NMi-2254799-01\", date: \"2020-08-14\", pages: 235, role: \"type_evaluation_report\"}\n- {id: \"NMi-2254799-03\", date: \"2020-09-30\", pages: 44, role: \"type_evaluation_report\"} — hmm. The associated reports list — these are the reports containing tests and examinations. The Type Evaluation Report is presumably NMi-2254799-01 (biggest, includes everything) or the whole 2254799 series. Honestly, without more info, role assignment is guesswork. Options: mark all as \"test_report\", or first as type_evaluation_report. The certificate says \"represented by the sample(s) identified in the OIML Type Evaluation Report(s)\". The associated reports listed are likely the TER(s). NMi convention: the Type Evaluation Report number often matches project. NMi-2254799 = TER (parts 01, 03, 04), and NMi-2564429, NMi-2496280, NMi-2563125 are later test reports (modifications/extensions). I'll go: 2254799-01/-03/-04 → type_evaluation_report; others → test_report. It's a judgment call, acceptable.\n\n- documentation folder: T11851-9 → include as test_reports entry with role \"documentation_file\", id \"T11851-9\", no date/pages? Schema fields: id, date, pages, role. date/pages omit. I'll include {id: \"T11851-9\", role: \"documentation_file\"}.\n\nfootnotes: none in fragment. The \"*\" wildcard note is not a footnote per se... Actually it kind of is: \"* With X being 1 for standard...\" — it's an asterisk note explaining wildcard. Should I capture as footnote? footnotes[]: marker \"*\", text: \"With X being 1 for standard or 3 for advanced or 5 for premium (mobile Comms - MC) variant; Y being 1, 2, 8 or 9 (integral/remote version, optionally sensor only); Z being R (reduced bore), V (virtual full bore) or F (full bore).\" That seems reasonable and useful. I'll include it.\n\ncharacteristics — final structure:\n\ntype_level:\n- measuring_principle: {value: \"Electromagnetic\", unit_symbol: null}\n- accuracy_class: {value: \"1 and 2\"} — hmm, \"1 and 2\". Or value: \"1 / 2\"? From table: \"1 and 2\". Use that.\n\nWait, should accuracy class be model_level? Both tables same. Type level fine.\n\n- environmental_class: {value: \"O\"}\n  plus environment_note \"installed outdoors\"? I'll do value: \"O (installed outdoors)\". Strings fine.\n- mechanical_class: {value: \"M1\"}\n- ambient_temperature_range: {value: {min: -25, max: 55}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"} — label canonical: \"temperature_range_ambient\" → \"ambient_temperature_range\". Rule examples convert to snake_case; I'll use \"ambient_temperature_range\".\n- water_temperature_class: {value: \"T50\"}\n- water_temperature_range: {value: {min: 0.1, max: 50}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: {value: 1.6, unit_symbol: \"MPa\"}\n- orientation: {value: \"All positions (horizontal, vertical or diagonal)\"} — keep as in source: \"All positions (Horizontal, vertical, or diagonal)\". Keep verbatim-ish.\n- reverse_flow: {value: \"The sensor is intended to measure reverse flow\"} — hmm trailing punctuation removed. \"Sensor intended to measure reverse flow\"? Keep original: \"The sensor is intended to measure reverse flow\".\n- checking_facilities: {value: \"Type P (permanent) automatic checking facility\"}\n- ac_mains_voltage: {value: {min: 95, max: 240}, unit_symbol: \"V\"} — label: \"ac_power_supply_voltage\".\n- ac_power_supply_frequency: {value: {min: 50, max: 60}, unit_symbol: \"Hz\"}\n- dc_power_supply_voltage (renewable energy): {value: {min: 6, max: 32}, unit_symbol: \"V\"} — label \"renewable_energy_dc_supply_voltage\"? I'll use \"dc_power_supply_voltage\" and maybe note. Simpler: \"renewable_energy_power_supply_voltage\". Hmm. Keep \"dc_power_supply_voltage\".\n- battery_voltage: {value: 3.6, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n- battery_operating_voltage_range: {value: {min: 2.9, max: 3.6}, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n\nmodel_level:\n- electromagnetic_environment: values: [{model: \"FEW41Y.Z\", value: \"E2\"}, {model: \"FEW43Y.Z\", value: \"E2\"}, {model: \"FEW45Y.Z\", value: \"E1 with external antenna cable, E2 without external antenna cable\"}]\n\nHmm — should the structured value include unit fields? Schema shows model_level values {model, value, footnote_markers[]}. unit_symbol at attribute level. Fine.\n\n- replaceable_battery_count: values: [{FEW41Y.Z: 2}, {FEW43Y.Z: 2}, {FEW45Y.Z: 6}] unit null. And note FEW45 AC/DC supplies include 4x 3.6 V batteries — could add attribute \"auxiliary_replaceable_batteries_with_ac_dc_supply\": only for FEW45, count 4. Model_level entry: values [{FEW45Y.Z, 4}]. Hmm, is that over-engineering? It's in the source. I'll include as \"batteries_included_with_ac_dc_supply\" maybe skip. Let me include it as model_level attribute \"replaceable_batteries_included_with_mains_or_dc_supply\": values [{FEW45Y.Z, value: 4}]. Actually count: \"4x 3.6 V replaceable batteries\" — value 4, unit null, maybe note voltage 3.6V. Fine, include.\n\nconfig_level:\n- pressure_loss: attribute \"pressure_loss\", axis: \"bore type and nominal size (DN)\", values as above with Δp class in condition.\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: could be config_level too: axis \"bore type and nominal size\": [{condition: \"reduced bore and full bore below DN250\", value: \"U0 / D0\"}, {condition: \"full bore DN250 and larger\", value: \"U3 / D0\"}]. But careful: \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream), except for full bore sizes DN250 and larger which have U3 and D0\". So default U0/D0; exception full bore ≥DN250 U3/D0. I'll do config_level:\n  values: [{condition: \"default (0 x DN upstream, 0 x DN downstream)\", value: \"U0 and D0\"}, {condition: \"full bore sizes DN250 and larger (3 x DN upstream, 0 x DN downstream)\", value: \"U3 and D0\"}]\n\nHmm, condition \"default\" is awkward. Alternatively keep it type_level as descriptive string. I think config_level with conditions: \"Reduced bore and full bore below DN250\" and \"Full bore DN250 and larger\". The first is my inference — \"default\" is what source says (\"except for\"). I'll use condition: \"Default (all sizes)\" hmm. Let me write: {condition: \"Default\", value: \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream, 0 x DN downstream)\"}, {condition: \"Full bore sizes DN250 and larger\", value: \"U3 and D0 (3 x DN upstream, 0 x DN downstream)\"}. Good.\n\nAlso the straight lengths are embedded. OK.\n\nNow — pressure loss: source: \"Reduced bore: For sizes DN40 and DN50: Δp 40 (0,40 bar); For sizes larger than DN50: Δp 63 (0,63 bar). (Virtual) Full bore: DN40 & DN50: Δp 10 (0,10 bar); DN65 to DN200: Δp 16 (0,16 bar); DN250 and larger: Δp 10 (0,10 bar)\".\n\nconfig_level:\n- attribute: \"pressure_loss\", axis: \"bore_type_and_nominal_size\", values:\n  - {condition: \"Reduced bore, DN40 and DN50 (pressure loss class Δp 40)\", value: 0.40, unit_symbol: \"bar\"}\n  - {condition: \"Reduced bore, larger than DN50 (pressure loss class Δp 63)\", value: 0.63, unit_symbol: \"bar\"}\n  - {condition: \"Virtual or full bore, DN40 and DN50 (pressure loss class Δp 10)\", value: 0.10, unit_symbol: \"bar\"}\n  - {condition: \"Virtual or full bore, DN65 to DN200 (pressure loss class Δp 16)\", value: 0.16, unit_symbol: \"bar\"}\n  - {condition: \"Virtual or full bore, DN250 and larger (pressure loss class Δp 10)\", value: 0.10, unit_symbol: \"bar\"}\n\nWait — \"Virtual) Full bore\" — the cert says \"(Virtual) Full bore\" meaning virtual full bore and full bore. I'll write \"Virtual or full bore\".\n\nNumbers: 0,40 → 0.4; 0,63 → 0.63; 0,10 → 0.1; 0,16 → 0.16. Use 0.4, 0.63, 0.1, 0.16, 0.1.\n\nBattery: \"2x 3,6 V\" → count 2, voltage 3.6. Battery count model-level.\n\nNow models list — also should I include FET4* sensor as a model? The components handle it. Models: FEW41Y.Z, FEW43Y.Z, FEW45Y.Z.\n\nWait, but is the model axis really the transmitter? Tables 1 and 2 both describe the whole instrument \"AquaMaster4 FEW41Y.Z\" etc. Yes, model_id per transmitter type works.\n\nAlso Y variants: attributes could note \"y: 1, 2, 8 or 9\". I'll add attributes: {\"y_variants\": \"1, 2, 8 or 9 (integral/remote, optionally sensor only)\", \"z_variants\": \"R (reduced bore), V (virtual full bore), F (full bore)\"} to each model? That's repetitive. Maybe put in family-level? model_family schema only has family_name and models[]. I could add an extra key \"wildcards\" — deviating from schema. Better keep in certified_type.description (already) and not repeat per model. Actually per-model attributes: x: 1, x_label: \"standard\". Good.\n\nLet me also double check page_total: \"Page 1 of 13\" → 13.\n\ndate_issued: \"5 June 2025\" → \"2025-06-05\".\n\nPerson responsible: \"— M.Ph.D. Schmidt\" → \"M.Ph.D. Schmidt\".\n\nScheme A: \"This OIML Certificate is issued under scheme A.\" → scheme \"A\".\n\ncertificate.oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\".\n\nNow assemble JSON. Ensure valid JSON, no trailing commas, numbers not strings for numerics.\n\ncharacteristics.type_level format: `<label>: {value, unit, footnote_markers[]}` — schema says {value, unit, footnote_markers[]}? In rule 2 the shape is {value, unit_symbol, unit_id, footnote_markers}. The schema YAML says `<label>: {value, unit, footnote_markers[]}` but rule 2 (STRICT) says unit_symbol and unit_id. I'll follow rule 2: include value, unit_symbol, unit_id (null where unsure). Should I include unit_id: null everywhere? Rule 3: \"Leave unit_id: null unless you are certain of the unitsdb ID.\" I know some: °C → \"u:degree_Celsius\" (given in example). V, Hz, MPa? The example gives \"u:degree_Celsius\", \"u:...\"? The example unit_ids shown: \"u:degree_Celsius\". I'm not certain of others (e.g., MPa might be \"u:megapascal\", Hz \"u:hertz\", V \"u:volt\", bar \"u:bar\", t \"u:tonne\"). To be safe, set unit_id null except °C where the example explicitly gives \"u:degree_Celsius\". I'll use that for temperature entries.\n\nFor model_level entries: attribute, unit_symbol, values[{model, value, footnote_markers?}]. Include unit_symbol at attribute level. For electromagnetic_environment unit_symbol null. Fine.\n\nLet me now decide on omitting unit_id or including null. Including \"unit_id\": null explicitly is fine and consistent.\n\nStructure:\n\n{\n  \"certificate\": {\n    \"number\": \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\",\n    \"scheme\": \"A\",\n    \"project_number\": \"3939609\",\n    \"page_total\": 13,\n    \"member_state\": \"The Netherlands\",\n    \"date_issued\": \"2025-06-05\",\n    \"oiml_issuer_id\": \"NL1\"\n  },\n  \"issuing_authority\": {\n    \"name\": \"NMi Certin B.V.\",\n    \"address_lines\": [\"Thijsseweg 11\", \"2629 JA Delft\", \"The Netherlands\"],\n    \"person_responsible\": \"M.Ph.D. Schmidt\",\n    \"person_title\": \"Certification Board\",\n    \"phone\": \"+31 88 636 2332\",\n    \"email\": \"certin@nmi.nl\",\n    \"website\": \"www.nmi.nl\",\n    \"oiml_issuer_id\": \"NL1\"\n  },\n  \"applicants\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"ABB Limited\",\n      \"address_lines\": [\"Oldends Lane\", \"GL10 3TA Stonehouse\", \"United Kingdom\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"manufacturers\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"ABB Limited\",\n      \"address_lines\": [\"Oldends Lane\", \"GL10 3TA Stonehouse\", \"United Kingdom\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"certified_type\": {\n    \"category\": \"Water meter\",\n    \"type_designations\": [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"AquaMaster4 FET4*\", \"FEW41Y.Z\", \"FEW43Y.Z\", \"FEW45Y.Z\"],\n    \"module_designation\": null,\n    \"description\": \"Electromagnetic water meter AquaMaster4 comprising flow transmitter FEW4XY.Z and flow sensor FET4*. X = 1 standard, 3 advanced, 5 premium (mobile Comms - MC) variant; Y = 1, 2, 8 or 9 (integral/remote version, optionally sensor only); Z = R (reduced bore), V (virtual full bore) or F (full bore)\"\n  },\n  \"model_family\": {\n    \"family_name\": \"AquaMaster4\",\n    \"models\": [\n      {\"model_id\": \"FEW41Y.Z\", \"label\": \"AquaMaster4 with FEW41 transmitter\", \"attributes\": {\"x\": 1, \"x_variant\": \"standard\"}},\n      {\"model_id\": \"FEW43Y.Z\", \"label\": \"AquaMaster4 with FEW43 transmitter\", \"attributes\": {\"x\": 3, \"x_variant\": \"advanced\"}},\n      {\"model_id\": \"FEW45Y.Z\", \"label\": \"AquaMaster4 with FEW45 transmitter\", \"attributes\": {\"x\": 5, \"x_variant\": \"premium (mobile Comms - MC)\"}}\n    ]\n  },\n  \"components\": [\n    {\n      \"role\": \"flow transmitter\",\n      \"type_designations\": [\"FEW41Y.Z\", \"FEW43Y.Z\", \"FEW45Y.Z\"],\n      \"alternatives\": null,\n      \"characteristics\": {}\n    },\n    {\n      \"role\": \"flow sensor\",\n      \"type_designations\": [\"FET4*\"],\n      \"alternatives\": null,\n      \"characteristics\": {}\n    }\n  ],\n  \"characteristics\": {\n    \"type_level\": {\n      \"measuring_principle\": {\"value\": \"Electromagnetic\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null},\n      \"accuracy_class\": {\"value\": \"1 and 2\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null},\n      \"environmental_class\": {\"value\": \"O (installed outdoors)\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null},\n      \"mechanical_class\": {\"value\": \"M1\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null},\n      \"ambient_temperature_range\": {\"value\": {\"min\": -25, \"max\": 55}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\", \"unit_id\": \"u:degree_Celsius\"},\n      \"water_temperature_class\": {\"value\": \"T50\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null},\n      \"water_temperature_range\": {\"value\": {\"min\": 0.1, \"max\": 50}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\", \"unit_id\": \"u:degree_Celsius\"},\n      \"maximum_admissible_pressure\": {\"value\": 1.6, \"unit_symbol\": \"MPa\", \"unit_id\": null},\n      \"orientation\": {\"value\": \"All positions (horizontal, vertical or diagonal)\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null},\n      \"reverse_flow\": {\"value\": \"The sensor is intended to measure reverse flow\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null},\n      \"checking_facilities\": {\"value\": \"Type P (permanent) automatic checking facility\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null},\n      \"ac_power_supply_voltage\": {\"value\": {\"min\": 95, \"max\": 240}, \"unit_symbol\": \"V\", \"unit_id\": null},\n      \"ac_power_supply_frequency\": {\"value\": {\"min\": 50, \"max\": 60}, \"unit_symbol\": \"Hz\", \"unit_id\": null},\n      \"dc_power_supply_voltage\": {\"value\": {\"min\": 6, \"max\": 32}, \"unit_symbol\": \"V\", \"unit_id\": null},\n      \"battery_voltage\": {\"value\": 3.6, \"unit_symbol\": \"V\", \"unit_id\": null},\n      \"battery_operating_voltage_range\": {\"value\": {\"min\": 2.9, \"max\": 3.6}, \"unit_symbol\": \"V\", \"unit_id\": null}\n    },\n    \"model_level\": [\n      {\n        \"attribute\": \"electromagnetic_environment\",\n        \"unit_symbol\": null,\n        \"values\": [\n          {\"model\": \"FEW41Y.Z\", \"value\": \"E2\"},\n          {\"model\": \"FEW43Y.Z\", \"value\": \"E2\"},\n          {\"model\": \"FEW45Y.Z\", \"value\": \"E1 (transmitter with external antenna cable) or E2 (transmitter without external antenna cable)\"}\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"attribute\": \"replaceable_battery_count\",\n        \"unit_symbol\": null,\n        \"values\": [\n          {\"model\": \"FEW41Y.Z\", \"value\": 2},\n          {\"model\": \"FEW43Y.Z\", \"value\": 2},\n          {\"model\": \"FEW45Y.Z\", \"value\": 6}\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"config_level\": [\n      {\n        \"attribute\": \"pressure_loss\",\n        \"axis\": \"bore type and nominal size (DN)\",\n        \"values\": [\n          {\"condition\": \"Reduced bore, DN40 and DN50 (class Δp 40)\", \"value\": 0.4, \"unit_symbol\": \"bar\"},\n          {\"condition\": \"Reduced bore, larger than DN50 (class Δp 63)\", \"value\": 0.63, \"unit_symbol\": \"bar\"},\n          {\"condition\": \"Virtual or full bore, DN40 and DN50 (class Δp 10)\", \"value\": 0.1, \"unit_symbol\": \"bar\"},\n          {\"condition\": \"Virtual or full bore, DN65 to DN200 (class Δp 16)\", \"value\": 0.16, \"unit_symbol\": \"bar\"},\n          {\"condition\": \"Virtual or full bore, DN250 and larger (class Δp 10)\", \"value\": 0.1, \"unit_symbol\": \"bar\"}\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"attribute\": \"flow_profile_sensitivity_class\",\n        \"axis\": \"bore type and nominal size (DN)\",\n        \"values\": [\n          {\"condition\": \"Default (0 x DN upstream, 0 x DN downstream)\", \"value\": \"U0 and D0\"},\n          {\"condition\": \"Full bore sizes DN250 and larger (3 x DN upstream, 0 x DN downstream)\", \"value\": \"U3 and D0\"}\n        ]\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  \"recommendation\": {\n    \"id\": \"R49-1\",\n    \"edition\": 2013,\n    \"scheme\": \"A\",\n    \"accuracy_classes\": [1, 2]\n  },\n  \"test_reports\": [\n    {\"id\": \"NMi-2254799-01\", \"date\": \"2020-08-14\", \"pages\": 235, \"role\": \"type_evaluation_report\"},\n    {\"id\": \"NMi-2254799-03\", \"date\": \"2020-09-30\", \"pages\": 44, \"role\": \"type_evaluation_report\"},\n    {\"id\": \"NMi-2254799-04\", \"date\": \"2020-12-24\", \"pages\": 180, \"role\": \"type_evaluation_report\"},\n    {\"id\": \"NMi-2564429-01\", \"date\": \"2021-08-02\", \"pages\": 26, \"role\": \"test_report\"},\n    {\"id\": \"NMi-2496280-01\", \"date\": \"2021-08-26\", \"pages\": 29, \"role\": \"test_report\"},\n    {\"id\": \"NMi-2496280-02\", \"date\": \"2021-11-23\", \"pages\": 25, \"role\": \"test_report\"},\n    {\"id\": \"NMi-2563125-01\", \"date\": \"2023-09-22\", \"pages\": 94, \"role\": \"test_report\"},\n    {\"id\": \"T11851-9\", \"role\": \"documentation_file\"}\n  ],\n  \"revision_history\": [\n    {\"revision\": \"13\", \"date\": \"2025-06-05\"}\n  ],\n  \"footnotes\": [\n    {\n      \"marker\": \"*\",\n      \"text\": \"With X being 1 for standard or 3 for advanced or 5 for premium (mobile Comms - MC) variant; Y being 1, 2, 8 or 9 (integral/remote version, optionally sensor only); Z being R (reduced bore), V (virtual full bore) or F (full bore)\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n\nWait — accuracy_class type_level value \"1 and 2\" — rule: numbers not strings... but it's two classes, a list. Schema value: number | {min,max} | string. \"1 and 2\" as string is the pragmatic choice; also recommendation.accuracy_classes [1,2]. OK.\n\nShould the FEW45 \"including 4x 3.6 V replaceable batteries\" for AC/DC supplies be captured? Add model_level attribute \"replaceable_batteries_included_with_ac_or_dc_supply\": values [{FEW45Y.Z, 4}]. I'll include it for completeness. Hmm, name: \"auxiliary_battery_count_with_ac_dc_supply\". Let me name it \"replaceable_batteries_included_with_mains_or_renewable_supply\". Eh, keep \"batteries_included_with_ac_dc_power_supply\". I'll include.\n\nAlso for FEW45 battery: \"6x 3,6 V\". Battery voltage 3.6 V for all. Good.\n\nDouble-check water temp: \"T50 (+0,1 °C / +50 °C)\" → min 0.1, max 50. Yes.\n\nMAP: \"1,6 MPa (16 bar)\". value 1.6 MPa. Good.\n\nPower AC: \"95 –240 VAC @ 50/60 Hz\" → 95–240 V, 50–60 Hz. Good.\n\nAmbient: \"-25 °C / +55 °C\" → min -25, max 55. Good.\n\nDC: \"6 – 32 V\". Good.\n\nBattery operating: \"2,9 - 3,6 V\" → 2.9–3.6. Good.\n\nDate formats: dates like \"14 August 2020\" → \"2020-08-14\". \"24 December 2020\" → \"2020-12-24\". \"30 September 2020\" → \"2020-09-30\". \"2 August 2021\" → \"2021-08-02\". \"26 August 2021\" → \"2021-08-26\". \"23 November 2021\" → \"2021-11-23\". \"22 September 2023\" → \"2023-09-22\". Good.\n\nRevision history — maybe safer to omit since \"changes\" unknown and date association is inference? The certificate number includes \"revision 13\" and issue date 5 June 2025. I think including {revision: \"13\", date: \"2025-06-05\"} is fine and helpful.\n\nThe components' characteristics: empty objects — schema says components[]: role, type_designations[], alternatives, characteristics{}. Empty characteristics fine, or add temperature range to flow sensor+transmitter? Ambient temp applies to both — already type level. Leave empty {}.\n\nAlso should I include \"software_identification\" references? Omit — cross-references to tables 3/4 on later pages.\n\nmatrix_tables: none in this fragment (Tables 5-12 referenced by name only, content not present). Omit.\n\nOne more consideration: certified_type.category — \"Water meter\". Good.\n\nmodel_family: should models include sensor? I'll leave transmitters only; sensor captured in components.\n\nAlso note Table 2 says electromagnetic environment E1/E2 — for transmitter FEW45. And FEW41/43 → E2. Good.\n\nFinal check on JSON validity: no trailing commas, proper quoting, unicode chars (°C, Δp) fine in JSON.\n\nI'll also consider whether \"person_title\": \"Certification Board\" is right... The layout: \"Issuing Authority — NMi Certin B.V., OIML Issuing Authority NL1 / 5 June 2025 / Certification Board / NMi Certin B.V. / address\". It reads like signature: [date], [title: Certification Board], [org]. I'll keep it.\n\nOutput the JSON only.",
